Executable mixed-bitness DWARF fixtures
======================================

These are deliberately constructed Linux x86-64 executables, not unmodified
production binaries. Each ELF64 ET_EXEC contains GCC-generated i386 and
x86-64 functions and two compilation units with address sizes 4 and 8.
Both compilation-unit orders are provided for DWARF 4 and DWARF 5.

The entry point calls the x86-64 function with positive and negative inputs,
checks the results, switches to the Linux user compatibility code selector
0x23, and calls the i386 function with the same inputs. It checks those
results, returns through selector 0x33 to 64-bit mode, and exits through
the Linux x86-64 syscall interface. It checks the code selectors as well.
The stack and executable code are below 4 GiB. Exit 0 means both machine
execution modes and both compiler-generated functions passed their checks.
Exit 1 or 2 means the 64-bit or 32-bit checks failed, respectively.

No 32-bit libc, dynamic loader, kernel-mode code, or emulator is required.
Running or regenerating the fixtures requires Linux x86-64 with support for
user compatibility mode; unit tests only parse their bytes and are portable.
The fixed user selectors are defined in the Linux source:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/arch/x86/include/asm/segment.h

Rebuild all four fixtures and the independent expectations with:

    python test/testfiles_for_unittests/mixed_bitness/build.py

The generator uses GCC -O2 -m32/-m64 -gdwarf-4/-gdwarf-5 to produce assembly.
The i386 assembly is assembled with .code32 and GNU as --64. This preserves
the compiler's 4-byte DWARF addresses and emits ELF64 RELA relocations with
the proper addends. Do not replace this with objcopy -O elf64-x86-64 on an
ELF32 object: conversion of ELF32 REL relocations can lose implicit addends
in debug-section references, even when the resulting executable runs.

-gno-as-loc-support keeps the compiler-generated .debug_line addresses at
the CU's width rather than letting an ELF64 assembler infer 8-byte addresses.
-gno-variable-location-views makes the GNU readelf expectation extraction
independent of its formatting of location view pairs. -ffunction-sections
causes each CU to have a range list, and optimization produces three
location list references per CU. The mode switch source has no DWARF CU.
No DWARF section bytes are patched or copied from another executable.

build.py executes each fixture, requires clean GNU readelf location/range
and info dumps, and writes expected.json from those dumps without importing
pyelftools. There are eight list references per executable, 32 in total.
Tests compare every complete absolute interval and location expression with
those independent expectations, through direct lookup and list iteration.

The checked-in fixtures were generated using GCC 13.3.0 and GNU binutils
2.42 on Linux x86-64. Regeneration with another compiler/binutils version
can change instruction offsets, debug metadata, and expected.json. With
the same tools, file-prefix mapping removes the build-directory path.
LLVM dwarfdump 23.0.0 --verify reported no errors for all four fixtures;
DWARF 5 produced harmless duplicate file-name warnings from GCC's explicit
line-table output. GNU readelf 2.42 produced no warnings or errors.

This code and its fixture source are in the public domain.
